Why do Kubernetes Pods get stuck in Pending?
One of the first real Kubernetes problems I had to debug was a pod that simply refused to start.
No crash.
No error message in the container.
Just:
STATUS: Pending
This usually means the scheduler cannot place the pod on a node.
Some common causes I’ve seen:
• Insufficient CPU or memory on nodes
• Node selector mismatch
• Taints without tolerations
• Persistent volume not bound
• Resource quotas exceeded
When debugging, these are usually the first commands I check:
 
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl get events
kubectl describe node <node-name>
Most Kubernetes issues are not actually application problems.
They’re resource scheduling problems.
Understanding how the scheduler, nodes, and resources interact makes debugging much easier.
